[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "A degree (bachelor’s, master’s, or PhD) can be described as a work breakdown structure by decomposing the degree process into discrete pieces of work (e.g., applying to the program, completing coursework, passing exams, and fulfilling final requirements). In this framework, each activity has four parameters: the precursor, duration, due date, and endpoint. A precursor is an event or set of events that must occur before the activity can begin; it describes the set of conditions that allows the activity to begin. The duration is the length of time needed to complete the activity. The due date is the date by which the activity must be completed, frequently determined by contractual deadlines. Signifying that the activity has ended, the endpoint is usually a milestone or deliverable. We can illustrate the relationships among activities by using these parameters. In particular, we can draw an activity graph to depict the dependencies; the nodes of the graph are the project milestones, and the lines linking the nodes represent the activities involved. The chart helps the project manager to understand which activities can be performed concurrently, and also to see which items are on the critical path.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "A precursor is an event or set of events that must occur before the activity can begin; it describes the set of conditions that allows the activity to begin.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The duration is the length of time needed to complete the activity. The due date is the date by which the activity must be completed, frequently determined by contractual deadlines. Signifying that the activity has ended, the endpoint is usually a milestone or deliverable.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "We can illustrate the relationships among activities by using these parameters. In particular, we can draw an activity graph to depict the dependencies; the nodes of the graph are the project milestones, and the lines linking the nodes represent the activities involved.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The chart helps the project manager to understand which activities can be performed concurrently, and also to see which items are on the critical path.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "The text describes two primary organizational structures you might use: the chief programmer team, where one person is totally responsible for a system's design and development and all other team members report to the chief programmer, who has the final say on every decision, with an understudy and a librarian supporting documentation and preliminary testing; this division of labor allows the programmers to focus on what they do best: programming. An alternative is based on the idea of 'egoless' programming, where responsibility is distributed so everyone is equally responsible, the process is separated from the individuals, criticism targets the product or result rather than people, and the egoless team structure is democratic with all team members voting on decisions. Moreover, as teams grow, the need for a formal structure increases, though smaller teams (three or four members) may not always require elaborate organization. Your customer may also impose a structure, such as ensuring that the test team is totally independent of program design and development.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "One popular organizational structure is the chief programmer team, first used at IBM (Baker 1972).On a chief programmer team , one person is totally responsible for a system's design and development. All other team members report to the chief programmer, who has the final say on every decision.The chief programmer supervises all others, designs all programs, and assigns the code development to the other team members. Assisting the chief is an understudy, whose principal job is substituting for the chief programmer when necessary. A librarian assists the team, responsible for maintaining all project documentation. The librarian also compiles and links the code, and performs preliminary testing of all modules submitted to the library. This division of labor allows the programmers to focus on what they do best: programming."
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "an egoless approach holds everyone equally responsible. Moreover, the process is separated from the individuals; criticism is made of the  product  or  the  result, not  the  people  involved. The  egoless  team  structure  is democratic, and all team members vote on a decision, whether it concerns design considerations or testing techniques."
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Which structure is preferable? The more people on the project, the more need there is for a formal structure."
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Work styles are described as differing in how people interact and how decisions are made. Different people have different preferred styles for interacting with others on the job and for understanding problems that arise in the course of their work. Jung (1959) calls the former extroverts and the latter introverts.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Different people have different preferred styles for interacting with others on the job and for understanding problems that arise in the course of their work. For example, you may prefer to do a detailed analysis of all possible information before making a decision, whereas your colleague may rely on 'gut feeling' for most of his important decisions. You can think of your preferred work style in terms of two components: the way in which your thoughts are communicated and ideas gathered, and the degree to which your emotions affect decision making. Jung (1959) calls the former extroverts and the latter introverts ."
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "In addition, work styles can involve the choice of worker for a given task, with intuitive employees potentially preferring design and development (requiring new ideas) over maintenance programming and design (requiring attention to detail and analysis of complex results).",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Work styles can also involve choice of worker for a given task.For instance,intuitive employees may prefer design and development (requiring new ideas) to maintenance programming and design (requiring attention to detail and analysis of complex results)."
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Expert judgment is used to make effort predictions as part of planning and managing the project. It can be based on a manager's experience with similar projects and may be conducted using techniques such as predicting pessimistic, optimistic, and most likely values (the Delphi technique) or by forming top-down or bottom-up estimates and using analogies to past systems.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Many effort-estimation methods rely on expert judgment.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The complete estimate can be computed from either a top-down or a bottom-up analysis of what is needed.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The Delphi technique makes use of expert judgment in a different way. Experts are asked to make individual predictions secretly, based on their expertise and using whatever process they choose. Then, the average estimate is calculated and presented to the group. Each expert has the opportunity to revise his or her estimate, if desired. The process is repeated until no expert wants to revise.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Machine-Learning Methods use learned models (such as neural networks) trained on historical data to predict effort for future projects. Neural networks can represent interconnected units that model activities involved in producing a software product. They are trained by inputting data from past projects so the network can identify patterns and relationships that inform predictions.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "In  the  past, most  effort-  and  cost-modeling  techniques  have  relied  on  algorithmic methods. That  is, researchers  have  examined  data  from  past  projects  and  generated equations from them that are used to predict effort and cost on future projects. However, some researchers are looking to machine learning for assistance in producing good estimates.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "There are many ways for a neural network to produce its outputs. Some techniques involve looking back to what has happened at other nodes; these are called back-propagation techniques.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Neural networks are developed by 'training' them with data from past projects. Relevant data are supplied to the network, and the network uses forward and backward algorithms to 'learn' by identifying patterns in the data.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Two values that can be calculated from E (the estimate) and A (the actual) to assess prediction accuracy are:",
        "citations": [],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet_intro",
        "segment": "Acceptance range (the prediction window)",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "We describe this window by using an acceptance range : a statement of the maximum difference between prediction and actual value.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "We must always state in advance what range is acceptable before using a prediction system.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Define an acceptance range as the maximum allowed difference between the predicted value and the actual value; evaluate predictions by checking how many predictions fall within this window.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "We describe this window by using an acceptance range : a statement of the maximum difference between prediction and actual value.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "We must always state in advance what range is acceptable before using a prediction system.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Bias measure (how often actual observations are less than predicted)",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "We deal with bias by comparing how often the observed times of failure are less than the predicted ones.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Define a bias statistic as the empirical probability that A is less than E (the likelihood that the actual observation was less than what was predicted).",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "In other words, we estimate the likelihood that the actual observation was less than what we had predicted it would be.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

