[
  {
    "role": "bullet_intro",
    "segment": "For each module, determine whether cohesion is high or low and provide a brief rationale grounded in the definitions of cohesion.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Cohesion. In contrast to measuring the interdependence among multiple modules, cohesion refers to the dependence within and among a module's internal elements (e.g., data, functions, internal modules). The more cohesive a module, the more closely related its pieces are, both to each other and to the module's singular purpose.",
        "img_id": null
      }
      ,
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The worst degree of cohesion, coincidental, is found in a module whose parts are unrelated to one another. In this case, unrelated functions, processes, or data are combined in the same module for reasons of convenience or serendipity.",
        "img_id": null
      }
      ,
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "A module has logical cohesion if its parts are related only by the logic structure of its code.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "a) InventorySearchByID — Likely low cohesion because it mixes searching logic with returning a data structure of matching records; this blends data access, search criteria, and result formatting, which may reflect coincidental or logical cohesion issues.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The worst degree of cohesion, coincidental , is found in a module whose parts are unrelated to one another. In this case, unrelated functions, processes, or data are combined in the same module for reasons of convenience or serendipity.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "A module has logical cohesion if its parts are related only by the logic structure of its code.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "b) ProcessPurchase — Likely low cohesion because it performs multiple distinct responsibilities (removing inventory, printing a receipt, updating the log), which can be seen as multiple purposes within one module, risking the module evolving in different directions.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "A module that serves multiple purposes is at greater risk of its elements needing to evolve in different ways or at different rates.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The more cohesive a module, the more closely related its pieces are, both to each other and to the module's singular purpose.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "c) FindSet — Likely high cohesion if its steps (process user request, determine matching items, format list for display) are tightly related to producing a displayable result, i.e., a single purpose with related operations.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The more cohesive a module, the more closely related its pieces are, both to each other and to the module's singular purpose.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Sometimes a design is divided into modules that represent the different phases of execution: initialization, read input, compute, print output, and cleanup .",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Egoless means comments are directed at the process and the product, not at the participants.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "egoless, because comments are directed at the process and the product, not at the participants",
                "img_id": null
            }
            ,
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The review process encourages and enhances communication among the diverse members of the team.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Egoless reviews are necessary because it is far easier to change something in its abstract, conceptual stage than when it is already implemented, and early fault detection helps identify the root causes before they propagate.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "It is far easier to change something in its abstract, conceptual stage than when it is already implemented.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Much of the difficulty and expense of fixing faults late in development derive from tracking a fault to its source.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Steps to achieve egoless reviews include: the moderator leads the discussion but has no vested interest in the project itself; an impartial recorder is recruited to document what transpires and to ensure information is captured without taking sides; developers who are not involved with the project provide an outsider's perspective and can act as ad hoc verifiers, helping ensure the design is correct, consistent, and conforms to good design practice; and by participating, all in the review process assume equal responsibility for the design with the designers themselves.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The moderator leads the discussion but has no vested interest in the project itself. He or she encourages discussion, acts as an intermediary between opposing viewpoints, keeps the discussion moving, and maintains objectivity and balance in the process.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "the recorder does not get involved in the issues that arise; his or her sole job is to document what transpires. However, more than stenographic skills are required; the recorder must have enough technical knowledge to understand the proceedings and record relevant technical information.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Developers who are not involved with the project provide an outsider's perspective. They can be objective when commenting on the proposed design, because they have no personal stake in it. In fact, they may have fresh ideas and can offer a new slant on things.They also act as ad hoc verifiers, checking that the design is correct, is consistent, and conforms to good design practice. By participating in the review, they assume equal responsibility for the design with the designers themselves.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "I’ll describe a generic example you might relate to: a typical desktop word processor where the user interface feels cluttered and makes it hard to locate formatting options, especially when many toolbars are visible at once. This kind of issue relates to the usability guidance that the user interface should reside in its own software unit or layer to simplify customization for different audiences, and that some user-initiated commands (such as cancel, undo, aggregate, and show multiple views) require architectural support. The system should listen for these commands and, for commands like undo, maintain a chain of previous states so the user can return to a prior point. It should also be able to present multiple displays and keep them up-to-date and consistent as data change. These considerations show how design decisions about the UI can significantly affect the software’s architecture and its usability.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "the user interface should reside in its own software unit,or possibly its own architectural layer.This separation makes it easier to customize the user interface for different audiences",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "for the undo command, the system must maintain a chain of previous states to which to return.For the show multiple views command,the system must be able to present multiple displays and keep them up-to-date and consistent as data change",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

