[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Encapsulation isolates components that were developed separately, which can support reuse by grouping functionality into self-contained units that can be reused in other contexts.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Encapsulation isolates components that were developed separately.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "However, even with reuse, testing considerations remain: a program that has been adequately tested in isolation may not be adequately tested in combination, and if a subclass is modified or a new subclass added, previously inherited methods may need retesting. This cautions against assuming reuse automatically minimizes testing effort.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "a program that has been adequately tested in isolation may not be adequately tested in combination",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Information hiding supports reuse by guiding decomposition so that a unit encapsulates a separate design decision that could be changed in the future, and by using interfaces to describe each software unit in terms of its externally visible properties.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Information hiding (Parnas 1972) aims to make the software system easier to maintain. It is distinguished by its guidance for decomposing a system: each software unit encapsulates a separate design decision that could be changed in the future.Then we use the interfaces  and  interface  specifications  to  describe  each software  unit  in  terms  of  its externally  visible  properties.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "When designing for reuse, ensure that changeable design decisions are not exposed via a unit's interface, so that consumers are insulated from internal changes (e.g., the data format should not be exposed in the module's interface).",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "If changeability of data format is a design decision, the data format should not be exposed in the module's interface.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "A better design would encapsulate the data in a single, separate software unit; the sorting",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "A better design would encapsulate the data in a single, separate software unit; the sorting",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

