[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Benefits of making books and rebinding separate subclasses of a general sale item class include the ability to share a common interface and promote code reuse, as the price computation can be implemented in a uniform way while still allowing service-specific details to vary. The text notes that abstracting commonality into an abstract Services class generalizes the different types of services (Fuel, Maintenance, and Parking) and that the price() method is common among all service classes, but the actual computation can be different depending on the service being purchased.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "we can create an abstract Services class  that  generalizes  the  different types of  services  ( Fuel , Maintenance , and Parking )  that  the station  offers.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "the price() method is common among all service classes, but  the  actual  computation  can  be  different, depending  on  which  service  is  being  purchased.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Additionally, treating both services as subclasses of a sale item furthers polymorphism, enabling the system to handle different sale items uniformly and to extend with new sale item types without changing clients that use them. The discussion about abstracting common behaviors (e.g., billing) indicates that such behaviors can be treated as class-wide responsibilities, which supports a uniform handling of different service types.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The behaviors will become actions or responsibilities taken by a class or object, or actions done to a class or object. For example, billing a customer is a behavior.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Possible arguments against using inheritance include the risk of over-generalization if rebinding is sufficiently different from other sale items, leading to a fragile base class. The text’s emphasis on commonalities being handled by an abstract Services class suggests a balance: only shared aspects (like a common price operation) belong in the base, while distinct behavior should stay in concrete subclasses to avoid unnecessary coupling.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The behaviors will become actions or responsibilities taken by a class or object, or actions done to a class or object.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Factors that could influence the decision include: whether the sale items truly share a stable interface and common operations (e.g., price) across item types, whether changes to one service type impact others, and the need for easy extension to add new sale item types. The example guiding questions about what data, attributes, and operations are applicable to a class, and how code can be simplified by shared abstractions, should guide whether inheritance is appropriate here.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The following questions can act as guidelines for what can be included in a list of candidate classes:\n - What data need to be 'processed' in some way?\n - What items have multiple attributes?\n - When do we have more than one object in a class?\n - What information is based on the requirements themselves, and not derived from our understanding of the requirements?\n - What attributes and operations are always applicable to a class or object?",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "we can create an abstract Services class  that  generalizes  the  different types of  services  ( Fuel , Maintenance , and Parking )  that  the station  offers.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

