[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "The relationship between design and implementation is one of direct correspondence: the program design components should map to the program code components, so that the design's modularity is carried forward into the code. It is essential that design characteristics such as low coupling, high cohesion, and well-defined interfaces become program characteristics, so that the algorithms, functions, interfaces, and data structures can be traced easily from design to code and back again. This correspondence is crucial because the system’s general purpose may remain the same, but its details may change over time, and changes are made first to the high-level design and then traced through lower design levels to the code that must be modified. In later chapters, we will see that testing, maintenance, and configuration management are impossible without the links established by these standards. The sooner a fault is identified in the design, the fewer places must be examined to locate its cause.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The most critical standard is the need for a direct correspondence between the program design components and the program code components.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Design characteristics, such as low coupling, high cohesion, and well-defined interfaces, should also be program characteristics, so that the algorithms, functions, interfaces, and data structures can be traced easily from design to code and back again.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Thus, the correspondence between design and code is essential.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
