[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "The objective of unit and integration testing was to ensure that the code implemented the design properly; that is, that the programmers wrote code to do what the designers intended. In system testing, we have a very different objective: to ensure that the system does what the customer wants it to do.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The objective of unit and integration testing was to ensure that the code implemented the design properly; that is, that the programmers wrote code to do what the designers intended.In system testing, we have a very different objective: to ensure that the system does what the customer wants it to do.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Unit testing verifies that components function properly in a controlled environment, and integration testing verifies that the system components work together as described; system testing then tests the system to ensure that it has the desired functionality.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "In developing a large system, testing usually involves several stages. First, each program component is tested on its own, isolated from the other components in the system. Such testing, known as module testing , component testing , or unit  testing , verifies  that  the component functions properly with the types of input expected from studying the component's design. Unit testing is done in a controlled environment whenever possible, so that the test team can feed a predetermined set of data to the component being tested and observe what output actions and data are produced. In addition, the test team checks the internal data structures, logic, and boundary conditions for the input and output data.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "When collections of components have been unit-tested, the next step is ensuring that the interfaces among the components are defined and handled properly. Integration testing is the process of verifying that the system components work together as described in the system and program design specifications.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Once we are sure that information is passed among components in accordance with the design, we test the system to ensure that it has the desired functionality.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Function testing checks that the integrated system performs its functions as specified in the requirements.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "There are several steps in testing a system:\n - function testing\n - performance testing\n - acceptance testing\n - installation testing",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "A function test checks that the integrated system performs its functions as specified in the requirements.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Performance testing is designed and administered by the test team, and the results are provided to the customer; it measures system performance against the objectives and nonfunctional requirements.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "System performance is measured against the performance objectives set by the customer as expressed in the nonfunctional requirements. For example, function testing may have demonstrated that a test system can calculate  the  trajectory  of  a  rocket, based on rocket thrust, weather conditions, and related sensor and system information. Performance testing  is  designed  and  administered  by  the  test  team, and  the results  are  provided  to  the  customer.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Acceptance testing aims to enable customers and users to determine if the system meets their needs and expectations, and it is written, conducted, and evaluated by the customers (with developer assistance only on request).",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The purpose of acceptance testing is to enable the customers and users to determine if the system we built really meets their needs and expectations.Thus, acceptance tests are written, conducted, and evaluated by the customers, with assistance from the developers only when the  customer  requests  an  answer  to a  technical  question.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "The text lists installation testing as one of the steps in testing a system, but does not specify its particular purpose.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "There are several steps in testing a system:\n - function testing\n - performance testing\n - acceptance testing\n - installation testing",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Verification ensures that we build the system right, while validation ensures that we build the right system.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Thus, to summarize, verification ensures that we build the system right , whereas validation ensures that we build the right system!",
        "img_id": null
      }
      ,
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "We verify that one document or artifact conforms to another. Thus, we verify that our code conforms to our design,and that our design conforms to our requirements specification;at the requirements level, we verify that our requirements specification conforms to the requirements definition.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "What kinds of testing address verification or validation is not enumerated as a fixed set in the provided text; the material describes verification as conformance between documents/artifacts (e.g., code to design, design to requirements) and validation as ensuring the requirements reflect stakeholders’ needs. Validation techniques depend on the stakeholders and the notations used, with some notations having tool support for checking consistency and completeness, and with reviews and problem-tracking aiding the process.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Our choice of validation technique depends on the experience and preferences of the stakeholders and on the technique's appropriateness for the notations used in the requirements definition.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Some notations have tool support for checking consistency and completeness. There are also tools that can help with the review process and with tracking problems and their resolutions.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "For example, some tools work with you and the customer to reduce the amount of uncertainty in the requirements.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The book's Web page points to requirements-related tools.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Cleanroom aims to certify software with respect to the specifications and to produce zero-fault or near-zero-fault software, applying these goals through a set of structured techniques that begin with box structures (black box, state box, then clear box) and proceed to convert clear-box specifications into intended functions via a correctness theorem that describes the relationship to control structures.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The principles are applied by blending several techniques discussed in this and earlier chapters. First, software is specified using box structures, introduced in Chapter 8. The system is defined as a black box, refined as a state box, and refined again as a clear box. The box structures encourage analysts to find omissions in requirements early in the life cycle, when they are easier and cheaper to fix.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "A correctness theorem defines a relationship, expressed as one of three correctness conditions, that describes the correctness of each intended function with respect to its control structures.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The final step involves statistical usage testing, where test cases are randomized based on probability of usage, as we saw earlier in this chapter.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "In this chapter, we explore several aspects of testing programs. We distinguish conventional testing approaches from the cleanroom method, and we look at how to test a variety of systems. We present definitions and categories of software problems, and we discuss  how  orthogonal  defect  classification  can  make  data  collection  and  analysis more effective. We then explain the difference between unit testing and integration testing.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

