[
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Training aids designed for operators to understand internal system operations are inappropriate for users, because user training is based primarily on major system functions and the user's need to access them, while only the operator is concerned with internal operations.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "User Training. Training for users is based primarily on major system functions and the user's need to access them.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "only the operator is concerned with them.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Documentation and training for infrequent users can be simplified to basic functions, whereas standard training for frequent users covers more features; applying the infrequent-user approach to a president who uses the system only occasionally would be inappropriate.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The president has no need to know all the special features of the system; training for infrequent users can address only basic document creation and modification functions.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "New users who may replace trained users require appropriate training adaptations; starting with basics and progressively introducing functions shows why a one-size-fits-all approach to training is unsuitable.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "However, new users may later replace trained users, often because of changing job assignments. Training must be available at these times to show them how the system works.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Common issues often come from documentation that is outdated or not read, with the observation that only 10% to 15% of users read the manual in an intensive training program, leaving others to struggle when questions arise.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "one study showed that only 10% to 15% of the users in an intensive training program read the manual at all",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "To reduce interference, users may benefit from alternative aids such as icons and online help, which can make functions clearer and easier to access than recalling commands and syntax.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "A click of the mouse selects an icon, and a second click invokes the function.Training for such systems is relatively simple, since the functions are more easily remembered by scanning the icons than by recalling commands and syntax.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Online help and hypermedia collections can further ease training by allowing users to dive into related documents as needed, reducing the need to search through paper manuals.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Similarly, online help makes training easier. The user can scan additional information about a function's purpose or use without having to take the time to search through paper documents. Sophisticated online help that makes use of hypermedia technology allows the user to delve into collections of related online documents for as much detail as needed for understanding.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "To avoid future interference, plan and keep training and documentation up to date from the start, integrate documentation with system software, and maintain channels to push updates (including known faults, work-arounds, new functionality, and reminders about training opportunities) to users.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "training and documentation should be planned and tracked from the project's beginning\n- training and documentation software should be integrated with the regular system software\n- all training and documentation modules and documents should take into account the varying needs of different audiences: users, operators, customers, programmers, and others who work with or interact with the system",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "updates to training and documentation can be planned early. The development team can put in place ways to access updates from a central location or to download them to users automatically from the Internet or Web sites. In particular, the documentation can be kept current, reporting on items such as known faults (and work-arounds),new functionality,new fault corrections, and other failure-related information.The updates can also remind users and operators of upcoming training courses, refresher courses, frequently asked questions, user group meetings, and other information useful to users, operators, and programmers.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    }
]

