[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "To compare English-language requirements with Z on the trial project, collect measurements of product quality attributes and process performance that reflect how well the requirements support development and maintenance (e.g., completeness, consistency, reliability, or maintainability) and use a quality framework such as the McCall model to interpret them. Also apply formal evaluation approaches by employing requirements measurement and Ardis's list to structure the assessment.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "we can examine a product to determine if it has desirable attributes.That is, we can ask whether a document, file, or system has certain properties, such as completeness, consistency, reliability, or maintainability. And we can use the notions of quality, such as the McCall model introduced in Chapter 1, to provide a framework for our questions.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Now that  you  have  finished  your  Loan Arranger  project, consider  how  you  would evaluate the software and the development process used to build it. How good is the software? What measures can you use to demonstrate its quality? How would you compare the quality of the Loan Arranger software with that of another project you have completed?",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Your clients  at  FCO  have  prepared  the  following  set  of  English-language  requirements for the Loan Arranger system. Like most sets of requirements, this set must be scrutinized in several ways to determine if it is correct, complete, and consistent. Using the  requirements  here  and  in  supplementary  material  about  the  Loan Arranger  in earlier chapters, evaluate and improve this set of requirements. Use many of the techniques presented in this  chapter, including  requirements  measurement  and Ardis's list. If  necessary, express  the  requirements  in  a  requirements  language  or  modeling technique, to  make  sure  that  the static  and  dynamic  properties  of  the  system  are expressed well.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Z (pronounced 'zed') is a formal requirements-specification language that structures set-theoretic  definitions  of  variables  into  a  complete  abstract-data-type  model  of  a problem, and uses logic to express the pre- and post-conditions for each operation.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Propose a formal experiment (a controlled study) to assess whether Z improves outcomes across multiple projects within the organization. This design aims to compare Z against a baseline under controlled conditions so that the findings can be generalized beyond a single project.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "One key motivator for using a formal experiment rather than a case study or survey is that the results of an experiment are usually more generalizable.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "As an alternative, consider a case study that samples from the variables by selecting values that are typical for the participating organization and its projects. Case studies are useful when you want to understand how Z behaves in a real-world setting within the organization's context.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "In a case study, we sample from the variables, selecting values that are typical for the participating organization and its projects.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "We should acknowledge the limitations inherent in these approaches: software engineering experiments are not like biology or chemistry experiments, and we must take into account the limitations and lack of control when deciding whether study results apply to a new situation.",
