        "segment": "Approach 1: Software process maturity and increased visibility",
        "citations": [],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "This approach emphasizes evaluating and improving software processes through maturity frameworks and awareness of their broader business impact. The text notes that “This study paints a very positive picture of software process improvement. However, we must be cautious about suggesting that the results indicate the general situation.” It also cautions that results should be viewed in context: “It is not clear how the reported results should be viewed in the larger context of business value.” Further, it highlights potential downsides of high maturity: “Sidebar 13.3 points out, there may be cases where high maturity can actually restrict business flexibility.” Empirical reliability concerns are raised: “Card (1992) reports that inconsistent results were obtained from CMM assessments of the same organization by different teams,” and methodological scrutiny is encouraged: “El Emam and Madhavji (1995) have further investigated reliability, asking … How reliable are such assessments?” These excerpts collectively frame a cautious, context-aware stance on process maturity as a path to improvement, rather than a universally applicable guarantee of benefits.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "This study paints a very positive picture of software process improvement. However, we must be cautious about suggesting that the results indicate the general situation.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "It is not clear how the reported results should be viewed in the larger context of business value.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Sidebar 13.3 points out, there may be cases where high maturity can actually restrict business flexibility.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Card (1992) reports that inconsistent results were obtained from CMM assessments of the same organization by different teams; thus, we must wonder how reliable the CMM assessments are.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "El Emam and Madhavji (1995) have further investigated reliability, asking",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Approach 2: Process modeling techniques and targeted evaluation of improvements",
        "citations": [],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "This approach centers on understanding and improving processes through modeling techniques and careful evaluation. The literature notes that “Eighteen different process modeling techniques were applied to the common problem, and varying degrees of satisfaction were found with each one.” The materials also point to established sources of process modeling activity, including the U.S. Software Engineering Institute (SEI), which “continues to investigate process modeling,” and European efforts, with descriptions of current research projects available at the European Commission’s portals. When evaluating improvements, the text emphasizes methodological care: “Use statistical techniques with care, because a single technique may not evaluate the true effect of process improvement.” It further notes that dramatic changes may be necessary for effects to appear in statistics: “In some cases, process improvement must be very dramatic if the quantitative effects are to show up in the statistical results.” These passages collectively underscore a modeling-driven, evidence-based pathway to process improvement that relies on multiple techniques and rigorous evaluation.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Eighteen different process modeling techniques were applied to the common problem, and varying degrees of satisfaction were found with each one.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The U.S. Software Engineering Institute (SEI) continues to investigate process modeling as part of its process improvement efforts.A list of its technical reports and activities can be found at http://www.sei.cmu.edu.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The European Community has long sponsored research in process modeling and a process model language. Descriptions of current research projects are available at http://cordis.europa.eu/fp7/projects_en.html.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Use statistical techniques with care, because a single technique may not evaluate the true effect of process improvement.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "In some cases, process improvement must be very dramatic if the quantitative effects are to show up in the statistical results.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    }
]

[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "The material describes the recognition-primed decision model as a description of how individual decision-makers use a repository of past examples and mental simulation to guide actions; it does not state that the model is intended to be applied directly to an entire organization.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "After watching and interviewing firefighters, emergency medical technicians, soldiers, and others who make important decisions under pressure, Klein has suggested a 'recognition-primed  decision  model,' as  shown  in Figure  14.6, to  describe  how  we really make decisions.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "He points out that we tend to keep a repository of examples in our heads, and we draw on these situations when we are confronted with a choice.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Complications for organizational use are suggested by the broader decision-making literature cited: context can bias a decision, and anchoring or optimism can distort judgments, which implies that an organizational experience base could inherit similar biases unless carefully managed.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Hershey, Kunreuther, and Schoemaker (1982) have demonstrated that the decision's context can bias the choice.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Anchoring-and-adjustment is another common technique employed by estimators. Here the estimator selects an analogous situation and then adjusts it to suit the new circumstances. However, there is considerable evidence to suggest that estimators are unduly cautious when making the adjustment. In other words, the anchoring dominates and then insufficient adaptation is made.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "A reluctance to appear negative can also affect expert judgment. DeMarco (1982) reminds us that 'realism can be mistaken for disloyalty,' leading to undue optimism in making predictions, both individually and in groups.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

